{% block content %}

  <header class="hw-hero">
    <p class="hw-crumbs"><a href="/">Home</a> &nbsp;/&nbsp; <a href="/blog/">Blog</a> &nbsp;/&nbsp; How to Watch</p>
    <p class="mc-kicker">For Guests</p>
    <h1>How to Watch a MomentCast Live Stream</h1>
    <p class="hw-dek">Someone sent you a link so you can be there for their big day. Here's everything you need, in about three minutes.</p>
    <p class="hw-meta">Updated October 24, 2026 &nbsp;&middot;&nbsp; 3 min read</p>
  </header>

  <!-- Anchor IDs are stable so a host can link a guest straight to an answer (#sound, #tv, #messages). -->
  <article class="hw-article">

    <div class="hw-quick">
      <p class="hw-quick-title">The short version</p>
      <ol>
        <li>Open the link on any phone, tablet, or computer. No app or account needed.</li>
        <li>If you see a countdown, leave the page open. The video starts on its own.</li>
        <li>Tap the speaker icon on the video to turn on the sound.</li>
        <li>Tap the fullscreen icon for the big picture.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>


    <h2 id="before">Before it starts</h2>

    <p>Open the link early and you'll see the event name, a countdown, and usually a photo of the couple or the guest of honor. Leave the page open. When the event goes live, the video appears by itself, usually within a minute.</p>

    <p>The date and time under the countdown are shown in the event's own time zone. If you're watching from another city or country, go by the countdown, not the clock time. The countdown is right no matter where you are.</p>

    <p>When the countdown reaches zero, the page says <span class="hw-screen">Event starting soon</span>. That means the host hasn't started the camera yet. Weddings run late. Keep the page open and it will switch over on its own.</p>


    <h2 id="sound">No sound? Tap the speaker</h2>

    <div class="hw-callout">
      <p>The video always starts muted. Phones and web browsers don't allow videos to play sound until you touch them. Tap the speaker icon on the video once and the sound comes on.</p>
    </div>

    <p>A small <span class="hw-screen">Tap to unmute</span> reminder shows for a few seconds when the video loads. If you missed it, look for the speaker icon in the controls along the bottom of the video. Tap the video once if the controls are hidden.</p>

    <p>Still nothing? Check that your phone isn't on silent and the volume is up. On an iPhone, the silent switch or Action Button can mute browser videos too.</p>


    <h2 id="watching">While you watch</h2>

    <p><strong>Go fullscreen.</strong> Tap the fullscreen icon in the video controls. On a phone, turning it sideways gives you the biggest picture.</p>

    <p><strong>The stream runs a little behind.</strong> What you see happens a few seconds or more after it happens in the room. Every live stream works this way, so if someone at the venue texts "they just kissed!" before you see it, nothing is wrong.</p>

    <p><strong>You can't rewind while it's live.</strong> Missed a moment? It will be in the replay after the event (<a href="#replay">see below</a>).</p>

    <p><strong>Share it with family.</strong> Below the video there's a QR code and the link. Tap the link to copy it, then paste it into a text. Someone sitting next to you can also scan the QR code with their phone camera.</p>


    <h2 id="tv">Watching on a TV</h2>

    <p>The easiest way is to mirror your phone or computer screen to the TV. Start the stream first, then:</p>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>iPhone or iPad:</strong> open Control Center, tap Screen Mirroring, and choose your Apple TV or AirPlay-compatible TV.</li>
      <li><strong>Android phone:</strong> open Quick Settings and look for Cast, Smart View, or Screen Cast. The name depends on the phone brand.</li>
      <li><strong>Computer with Chrome:</strong> open Chrome's menu (the three dots), choose Cast, then pick your TV or Chromecast.</li>
      <li><strong>Laptop with a cable:</strong> plug it into the TV with an HDMI cable and switch the TV to that input.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Then tap fullscreen. Keep the phone or computer plugged in and awake for the whole stream.</p>


    <h2 id="pauses">If the video stops</h2>

    <p>Live events have breaks, and the page tells you what's going on.</p>

    <p><span class="hw-screen">Stream paused</span> means the host stopped the camera for a while, maybe between the ceremony and the reception. While you wait, the most recent part plays again. When they go back live, the page switches over by itself.</p>

    <p><span class="hw-screen">Processing recording</span> means the last part is being saved. It usually takes a few minutes.</p>

    <p>The page checks for updates on its own, so most of the time you don't need to do anything. If it looks stuck for more than a few minutes, refresh the page. That fixes it almost every time.</p>


    <h2 id="replay">Watching the replay</h2>

    <p>Missed it live? Use the same link. A few minutes after the stream ends, the page plays the recording instead.</p>

    <p>If the host took breaks, the recording comes in parts. They play in order on their own, and the top of the video shows where you are, like <span class="hw-screen">Video 1 of 3</span>. You can skip forward and back inside each part. When a new part starts, you may need to tap the speaker icon again. Once every part has played, refresh the page to watch from the beginning.</p>

    <p>The replay stays up for 30 days after the event. After that the link shows <span class="hw-screen">Recording No Longer Available</span>.</p>


    <h2 id="messages">Messages you might see</h2>

    <table class="hw-table">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>On screen</th><th>What it means</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Event starting soon</td><td>The host hasn't started yet. Keep the page open.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Stream paused</td><td>The host is on a break. The page goes back to live by itself.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Processing recording</td><td>The last part is being saved. Give it a few minutes, then refresh if nothing changes.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Viewing Limit Reached</td><td>The event has used all the watching time the host set up. Let the person who sent you the link know. Once they add more time, the page lets you in on its own.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Event Not Found</td><td>The link is incomplete or mistyped. Ask for it again, or copy the whole thing.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>This event has ended</td><td>The event is over and no recording was saved.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Recording No Longer Available</td><td>The replay was available for 30 days and that time has passed.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>


    <h2 id="help">Still stuck?</h2>

    <ol>
      <li>Refresh the page.</li>
      <li>Try a different browser. Chrome and Safari both work well.</li>
      <li>Move closer to your Wi-Fi, or switch between Wi-Fi and cellular.</li>
    </ol>

    <p>For anything about the event itself, like the start time or when the replay will be ready, ask the person who sent you the link. They're running the stream and know the details.</p>

    <p class="hw-footnote">Streaming an event yourself? Read the <a href="/blog/livestream-event-guide">guide for photographers and videographers</a>.</p>

  </article>

